we take the position in our hospitals  very very seriously of course we do  but if you look at the figures yesterday  uh we had  about 80 percent of our intensive care  capacity occupied and that is not  unusual for this uh time of year but  we're not we're not in any way  complacent we have always put health  first  uh this year and we'll continue uh to do  that but health is about more than  controlling the virus k  if we go into a lockdown where we don't  support  people who are in the lowest paid  professions we'll have a mental health  crisis on top of a pandemic and  that's the point that i've been making  in standing up for those people  um why do you feel that westminster  doesn't  best understand the needs of the north  because when i put it to the minister he  said basically they're trying to save  lives  well so were we um and we've been  working with them all year to do that  and i'm very proud of the council  leaders here and everything  that our nhs has done we've worked  really really hard to do that but as i  said before  uh life is about more than the virus and  i think westminster sometimes struggles  to understand that people can't pay  two-thirds of their rent or um uh two  thirds of their bills you know  it's easier if you're on a a middle  income salary  um you can cope with living on two  thirds of it for a while but it's not  possible to do that if you're the lowest  paid and that let's just be really clear  about this okay  tier three lockdowns affect the lowest  paid people in society  people who work in pubs people who drive  taxis people who work on the doors in  pubs  these are the people that westminster  politicians traditionally  ignore but we are not going to do that  here if you're going to impose a  lockdown here  it's going to cause certain harm certain  harm to all of the people that i've i've  mentioned and that is why  we we have have stood firm because we  don't believe we can consign our  residents  uh to to hardship in that 